<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eichsfeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eichsfeld is a hilly historical region in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, now divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, around the towns of Heiligenstadt and Duderstadt. Acquired piece by piece by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> from the 11th century, it remained their territory until 1802 and stayed a Catholic island among Protestant lands, a character it keeps today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eichsfeld.jpg" alt="Eichsfeld" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. Leineweber (1845-1910) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Das_Eichsfeld_um_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
